---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

// Post for Linux photographers. Lightroom and most paid culling tools
// skip Linux, so this is a search phrase the site can rank for. The camera
// limits below must match the rawler column in docs/formats.astro.
---
<Base
  title="A fast RAW photo editor for Linux — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightPhotos is a free, open-source RAW photo culler and editor with native Linux builds for ARM and x86. Under 28MB, no account, no cloud, and edits that never touch your originals."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-08">October 8, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>A fast RAW photo editor for Linux</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Lightroom has no Linux app, and neither do most paid culling
          tools. LightPhotos ships native Linux builds for ARM and x86. It is
          free, open source, and under 28MB.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 2 min read</p>
      </header>

      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>Fast culling.</strong> Rate a shoot from the keyboard with 0&ndash;5 stars, pick and reject flags and colour labels, check focus at 100% with Space, and filter by rating, flag or label.</li>
        <li><strong>Non-destructive RAW editing.</strong> Exposure, tone, tone curve, white balance, vibrance, denoise, spot removal and Auto Adjust, rendered on the GPU.</li>
        <li><strong>Plain folders.</strong> No import and no database. Edits live in a <code>.lightphotos</code> folder beside your photos, so they back up and sync with everything else.</li>
        <li><strong>Export.</strong> Full-quality JPEGs at the size you choose, or straight to your own <a href="/blogs/exporting-to-your-own-immich-server.html">Immich server</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Private.</strong> The desktop app makes no network requests. See <a href="/privacy.html">Privacy</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it compares with other Linux tools</h2>
      <p>
        Linux already has excellent open-source photo editors. LightPhotos does
        not try to match their depth. It aims to be the quickest way through a
        shoot.
      </p>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">darktable</th>
              <th scope="col">RawTherapee</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Price</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Free, open source</td>
              <td>Free, open source</td>
              <td>Free, open source</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Best at</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Culling a shoot quickly and simple global edits</td>
              <td>Deep editing and library management</td>
              <td>Detailed RAW processing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Learning curve</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Minutes. A handful of sliders and shortcuts.</td>
              <td>Steep. Many modules and concepts.</td>
              <td>Moderate to steep</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Library</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Your folders, no import</td>
              <td>Import into a database</td>
              <td>File browser</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Masks and local edits</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Spot removal only</td>
              <td>Extensive</td>
              <td>Some</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Runs in a browser</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes, in Chrome</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>No</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Plenty of people will use both: cull in LightPhotos, then take the
        handful of keepers that need masks into darktable.
      </p>

      <h2>Requirements and limits</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Ubuntu 24.04+, Debian 13+, Fedora 40+, or similar, on x86-64 or ARM64.</li>
        <li>Distributed as a <code>.tar.gz</code>. Unpack and run, nothing to install system-wide.</li>
        <li>RAW decoding on Linux uses the open-source rawler decoder. Tested Sony, Canon, lossless Nikon, Fujifilm GFX, Panasonic, Pentax and Leica files open fully, as do iPhone ProRAW and Pixel DNGs.</li>
        <li>Fujifilm X-Trans files can be browsed and rated from their embedded preview but not yet edited. Nikon High Efficiency NEF, the OM System OM-5 II and Samsung Galaxy DNGs do not open. The full list is on <a href="/docs/formats.html">tested cameras</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Get it</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/downloads.html">Download for Linux</a>. The download page
        picks the right build for your CPU. Coming from Lightroom on another
        machine? See <a href="/blogs/lightroom-alternative.html">how LightPhotos
        compares with Lightroom</a>.
      </p>

